<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aapravasi Ghat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aapravasi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghat">Ghat</a> is the former immigration depot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-louis">Port Louis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, where indentured labourers arriving from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> were received from 1849 until the early 20th century. Nearly half a million workers passed through Mauritius under the indenture system after the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, shaping its population. The surviving buildings became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2006.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aapravasi-ghat.jpg" alt="Aapravasi Ghat" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suyash Dwivedi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aapravasi_Ghat_Museum,_Mauritius_(41).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
